cenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Support for George McGovern (1972)","body":"Barbra Streisand, despite her busy movie career, was convinced by Warren Beatty to perform at a benefit concert for George McGovern in Los Angeles on April 5th, 1972. She expressed no regrets for supporting McGovern, even after his landslide defeat, stating in 2010 at an American Film Institute award ceremony that she admired him and was glad to perform for his cause. \"I did get to be in one Warren Beatty production, the concert for George McOvern in 1972.\"","year":1972,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 21 - Rockin' the Vote - 70s Rock and Politics","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 21 - Rockin' the Vote - 70s Rock and Politics","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/021-rock-the-vote-52320-3-58-pm"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"MusicCares Person of the Year (2011) - Prestigious Honor","body":"Barbra Streisand was honored as the MusicCares Person of the Year at the 53rd annual Grammy Awards, a significant recognition, prior to presenting the Album of the Year award. \"earlier in the evening was fed with the music carous person of the year honor, which was a really big deal.\"","year":2011,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Who the Hell is Arcade Fire? Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q_c-m_NDznw"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Artwork Designer","body":"John Berg created art for Barbara Streisand. \"He also created art for Bob Dylan and Barbara Streisand.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Digital Debris Part 2: Album Artwork","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pwebM3TfSdg"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Musical Influence (1970s)","body":"Barbara Streisand was among the artists whose music Sinead O'Connor gravitated towards in her youth, alongside Bowie, Bob Marley, disco, and early punk. \"Later it was Bowie and Bob Marley and Disco in early punk, and Barbara Streisand.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Remembering Sinead O'Connor","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=afuqjWl1JyI"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Producer Shared with Dolly Parton","body":"Gary Klein, who produced Dolly Parton's music as she moved towards pop in the late 1970s, also worked with Barbra Streisand, indicating Parton's intentional effort to change her image and sound. \"She hired Gary Klein as her producer and he worked with Barbara Strysanne so that seems like Dolly was making a very intentional effort to change her image.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 23 - Women of Country Music in the 70s","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 23 - Women of Country Music in the 70s","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/023-country-women-72820-2-45-pm"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Transition to Rock-Pop Sound","body":"In the early 1970s, Barbra Streisand was actively trying to transition her musical style from her Broadway roots to a more contemporary rock-pop sound, exemplified by her performance of \"Stoney End\" at the 1972 McGovern benefit concert. \"This was a time when she was trying to transition from Broadway singer to more of this rock pop sound.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 21 - Rockin' the Vote - 70s Rock and Politics","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 21 - Rockin' the Vote - 70s Rock and Politics","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/021-rock-the-vote-52320-3-58-pm"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Tony Award for Best Actress in a Musical (1964)","body":"Nominated.","year":1964,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1537996"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"A